45485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442018 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>\\frac{3}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "056683d5-9c10-553b-93b1-01f7db0cfa24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442018 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{(3/4)t}.$ Thus, for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{(3/4)t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{3}{4})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}$ given $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nLet $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=$  {{field-1}}. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}=L\\{{{field-2}}\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)({{field-4}}){|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=$ {{field-5}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72ddd4c8-8e72-5f66-b964-d1ec00d699bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442019 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442020 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "954d3e87-b1ac-516e-bb73-13e2611b178e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442020 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{3t}.$ Thus, for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}$ given $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nLet $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ={{field-1}}\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}=L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={A}^{′}(t)\\text{ }dt \\\\ dv={{field-3}} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=$ {{field-5}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4855477-bdfa-5214-b5d7-5a97d7d6e1ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442021 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442022 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-4)},\\,s>4\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9ec2e0a-7b21-59e9-965b-b9f812cb11e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442022 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{4τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{4t}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
